1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,310
A 1-year-old watches a toy car roll

2
00:00:02,320 --> 00:00:04,270
behind a couch, and then they look at

3
00:00:04,280 --> 00:00:06,270
the far end of the couch waiting for it

4
00:00:06,280 --> 00:00:08,270
to come out. Nobody taught them that

5
00:00:08,280 --> 00:00:09,910
objects keep existing when they're

6
00:00:09,920 --> 00:00:12,070
hidden, or that moving things keep going

7
00:00:12,080 --> 00:00:13,750
in the direction they were headed. They

8
00:00:13,760 --> 00:00:15,830
picked it up just by watching long

9
00:00:15,840 --> 00:00:17,950
before they had words for any of it. And

10
00:00:17,960 --> 00:00:19,790
if the car doesn't come out, they stare

11
00:00:19,800 --> 00:00:21,750
for longer because the world just did

12
00:00:21,760 --> 00:00:23,590
something their internal model didn't

13
00:00:23,600 --> 00:00:25,430
predict. That's the kind of learning

14
00:00:25,440 --> 00:00:27,750
machines are still bad at. A large

15
00:00:27,760 --> 00:00:29,710
language model learns by predicting the

16
00:00:29,720 --> 00:00:31,990
next word billions of times over. And

17
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:33,710
that works well for text because the

18
00:00:33,720 --> 00:00:35,230
next word comes from a limited

19
00:00:35,240 --> 00:00:36,990
vocabulary, and the model can put a

20
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:39,350
probability on every option. Try the

21
00:00:39,360 --> 00:00:41,590
same trick on video by training a model

22
00:00:41,600 --> 00:00:43,470
to predict the next frame pixel by

23
00:00:43,480 --> 00:00:46,070
pixel, and it goes much worse. The model

24
00:00:46,080 --> 00:00:48,030
spends its capacity trying to guess the

25
00:00:48,040 --> 00:00:50,350
exact shape of every leaf moving in the

26
00:00:50,360 --> 00:00:52,510
wind and every ripple on a lake, which

27
00:00:52,520 --> 00:00:54,550
are details nobody could predict, and it

28
00:00:54,560 --> 00:00:56,550
ends up with blurry guesses and a weak

29
00:00:56,560 --> 00:00:58,230
sense of what actually happened in the

30
00:00:58,240 --> 00:01:01,510
scene. In 2022, Yann LeCun, who was

31
00:01:01,520 --> 00:01:03,870
Meta's chief AI scientist at the time

32
00:01:03,880 --> 00:01:05,470
and is one of the pioneers of deep

33
00:01:05,480 --> 00:01:07,790
learning, proposed a different answer to

34
00:01:07,800 --> 00:01:09,750
a question that sounds simple. When a

35
00:01:09,760 --> 00:01:11,830
machine learns by predicting, what

36
00:01:11,840 --> 00:01:14,350
exactly should it predict? His answer is

37
00:01:14,360 --> 00:01:16,870
an architecture called Jeppa, short for

38
00:01:16,880 --> 00:01:19,390
joint embedding predictive architecture.

39
00:01:19,400 --> 00:01:21,310
It's the basis of a line of models from

40
00:01:21,320 --> 00:01:23,430
Meta that learn from images and video

41
00:01:23,440 --> 00:01:25,830
without any labels, and one of them now

42
00:01:25,840 --> 00:01:28,350
lets a robot arm plan its own actions in

43
00:01:28,360 --> 00:01:30,950
a lab it's never seen. The idea behind

44
00:01:30,960 --> 00:01:33,590
Jeppa fits in one sentence, but to see

45
00:01:33,600 --> 00:01:36,030
why it works and why it very nearly

46
00:01:36,040 --> 00:01:38,270
doesn't, we have to start with what goes

47
00:01:38,280 --> 00:01:41,270
wrong when a model predicts pixels.

48
00:01:41,280 --> 00:01:43,470
The setting is called self-supervised

49
00:01:43,480 --> 00:01:45,550
learning, and the recipe is always the

50
00:01:45,560 --> 00:01:47,990
same. You take some data, hide part of

51
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:49,910
it, and train a network to fill in the

52
00:01:49,920 --> 00:01:52,230
missing part from the part it can see.

53
00:01:52,240 --> 00:01:54,230
Nobody has to label anything because the

54
00:01:54,240 --> 00:01:56,990
data supervises itself, and the hope is

55
00:01:57,000 --> 00:01:59,110
that to get good at filling in the gaps,

56
00:01:59,120 --> 00:02:01,110
the network has to learn something real

57
00:02:01,120 --> 00:02:03,310
about how the data is structured. Call

58
00:02:03,320 --> 00:02:05,830
the visible part X and the hidden part

59
00:02:05,840 --> 00:02:08,029
Y, and the whole question becomes how

60
00:02:08,039 --> 00:02:10,710
you score the network's guess about Y.

61
00:02:10,720 --> 00:02:12,830
The most direct approach is generative,

62
00:02:12,840 --> 00:02:14,590
which means the network outputs Y

63
00:02:14,600 --> 00:02:16,710
itself, every [snorts] pixel of it, and

64
00:02:16,720 --> 00:02:18,670
you measure how far its pixels are from

65
00:02:18,680 --> 00:02:21,110
the real ones. Masked autoencoders work

66
00:02:21,120 --> 00:02:23,230
this way, hiding most of an image and

67
00:02:23,240 --> 00:02:25,310
reconstructing the missing patches. The

68
00:02:25,320 --> 00:02:27,350
trouble shows up as soon as Y is

69
00:02:27,360 --> 00:02:29,750
uncertain. Picture a video of a person

70
00:02:29,760 --> 00:02:32,070
walking toward a fork in a path, and ask

71
00:02:32,080 --> 00:02:34,310
the model for the frame 2 seconds later,

72
00:02:34,320 --> 00:02:35,990
when they might have gone left or might

73
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:38,310
have gone right. A pixel loss like mean

74
00:02:38,320 --> 00:02:40,550
squared error rewards the single guess

75
00:02:40,560 --> 00:02:42,590
that's closest on average to every

76
00:02:42,600 --> 00:02:44,910
possibility, and the average of a person

77
00:02:44,920 --> 00:02:46,990
on the left and a person on the right is

78
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:49,590
a faint ghost on both sides. That's why

79
00:02:49,600 --> 00:02:51,710
pixel predictors produce blur whenever

80
00:02:51,720 --> 00:02:53,790
the future isn't certain. The second

81
00:02:53,800 --> 00:02:56,470
problem is quieter, but just as costly.

82
00:02:56,480 --> 00:02:58,630
A pixel loss treats every pixel as

83
00:02:58,640 --> 00:03:00,870
equally important, so the exact texture

84
00:03:00,880 --> 00:03:02,870
of a carpet counts as much as where the

85
00:03:02,880 --> 00:03:04,949
ball went. The network spends real

86
00:03:04,959 --> 00:03:07,110
capacity modeling detail that doesn't

87
00:03:07,120 --> 00:03:08,990
help it understand the scene, and some

88
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:11,070
of that detail, like noise in a camera

89
00:03:11,080 --> 00:03:13,229
sensor or leaves in the wind, can't be

90
00:03:13,239 --> 00:03:15,470
predicted at all, however good the model

91
00:03:15,480 --> 00:03:17,470
gets. So, you'd like to keep the

92
00:03:17,480 --> 00:03:19,470
fill-in-the-blank recipe, but stop

93
00:03:19,480 --> 00:03:21,830
asking the network for every pixel. What

94
00:03:21,840 --> 00:03:23,630
you really want is for it to predict

95
00:03:23,640 --> 00:03:25,750
only the parts of Y that are actually

96
00:03:25,760 --> 00:03:27,790
predictable, and to decide for itself

97
00:03:27,800 --> 00:03:29,630
which parts those are. That sounds

98
00:03:29,640 --> 00:03:31,310
impossible to write down as a loss

99
00:03:31,320 --> 00:03:33,470
function, until you move the prediction

100
00:03:33,480 --> 00:03:36,310
somewhere else. Jeppe's move is to stop

101
00:03:36,320 --> 00:03:38,110
predicting Y and predict a

102
00:03:38,120 --> 00:03:41,509
representation of Y instead. Both X and

103
00:03:41,519 --> 00:03:43,870
Y go through encoders, which are neural

104
00:03:43,880 --> 00:03:46,190
networks that turn raw input into a list

105
00:03:46,200 --> 00:03:48,190
of numbers called an embedding. The

106
00:03:48,200 --> 00:03:50,990
context encoder turns the visible part X

107
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:53,750
into an embedding we'll call S sub X,

108
00:03:53,760 --> 00:03:55,630
and the target encoder turns the hidden

109
00:03:55,640 --> 00:03:59,150
part Y into its own embedding, S sub Y.

110
00:03:59,160 --> 00:04:01,550
Then, a third network, the predictor,

111
00:04:01,560 --> 00:04:04,510
takes S sub X and tries to output S sub

112
00:04:04,520 --> 00:04:06,830
Y. The loss is simply the distance

113
00:04:06,840 --> 00:04:08,550
between the predicted embedding and the

114
00:04:08,560 --> 00:04:10,790
actual one. So, no pixels are ever

115
00:04:10,800 --> 00:04:12,750
reconstructed, and nothing is ever

116
00:04:12,760 --> 00:04:15,310
decoded back into an image. That one

117
00:04:15,320 --> 00:04:17,270
change deals with both problems from

118
00:04:17,280 --> 00:04:19,069
before. Because the target is an

119
00:04:19,079 --> 00:04:21,190
embedding the system produces itself,

120
00:04:21,200 --> 00:04:23,230
the target encoder is free to leave out

121
00:04:23,240 --> 00:04:25,190
whatever can't be predicted. If the

122
00:04:25,200 --> 00:04:26,909
leaves in the wind are random, the

123
00:04:26,919 --> 00:04:28,830
encoder can learn to drop them, and then

124
00:04:28,840 --> 00:04:30,750
there's nothing about them to get wrong.

125
00:04:30,760 --> 00:04:32,590
The prediction is only judged on what

126
00:04:32,600 --> 00:04:34,390
survives in the embedding, and that

127
00:04:34,400 --> 00:04:36,350
pushes the whole system toward features

128
00:04:36,360 --> 00:04:37,909
that are both informative about the

129
00:04:37,919 --> 00:04:40,630
scene and predictable from context, like

130
00:04:40,640 --> 00:04:42,510
where the objects are, what shape they

131
00:04:42,520 --> 00:04:44,230
have, and how they're moving. The

132
00:04:44,240 --> 00:04:46,510
predictor usually gets one more input,

133
00:04:46,520 --> 00:04:48,030
which tells it what it's being asked

134
00:04:48,040 --> 00:04:50,430
about. In images, that's the location of

135
00:04:50,440 --> 00:04:51,990
the hidden region. So, the predictor

136
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:53,710
knows it's filling in the top left

137
00:04:53,720 --> 00:04:55,670
corner and not the bottom right. In

138
00:04:55,680 --> 00:04:57,830
video, it can be how far ahead in time

139
00:04:57,840 --> 00:04:59,790
to predict, and later on, it'll be the

140
00:04:59,800 --> 00:05:02,030
action a robot takes. You can also give

141
00:05:02,040 --> 00:05:04,670
it a latent variable, usually written Z,

142
00:05:04,680 --> 00:05:06,390
which stands for whatever the context

143
00:05:06,400 --> 00:05:08,430
can't pin down, like whether the person

144
00:05:08,440 --> 00:05:10,510
at the fork turned left or right, so

145
00:05:10,520 --> 00:05:12,350
that different values of Z lead to

146
00:05:12,360 --> 00:05:14,390
different plausible futures instead of

147
00:05:14,400 --> 00:05:16,950
one blurred average. The name describes

148
00:05:16,960 --> 00:05:18,990
all of this. It's a joint embedding

149
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:21,510
architecture because X and Y are both

150
00:05:21,520 --> 00:05:23,430
embedded, and it's predictive because

151
00:05:23,440 --> 00:05:24,990
one embedding is predicted from the

152
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:26,750
other. But, once you write the design

153
00:05:26,760 --> 00:05:29,350
out like that, it has a flaw so obvious

154
00:05:29,360 --> 00:05:31,750
that it's surprising it works at all.

155
00:05:31,760 --> 00:05:34,390
The flaw is called collapse. The loss

156
00:05:34,400 --> 00:05:36,390
only asks that the predicted embedding

157
00:05:36,400 --> 00:05:38,470
be close to the target embedding, and

158
00:05:38,480 --> 00:05:39,990
both of those come from networks we're

159
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:42,190
training. That means the easiest way to

160
00:05:42,200 --> 00:05:44,630
get zero loss is for both encoders to

161
00:05:44,640 --> 00:05:47,110
ignore their input completely and output

162
00:05:47,120 --> 00:05:49,630
the same constant vector every time. The

163
00:05:49,640 --> 00:05:51,030
predictor learns to output that

164
00:05:51,040 --> 00:05:53,030
constant, too. The distance drops to

165
00:05:53,040 --> 00:05:54,830
zero, and the network has learned

166
00:05:54,840 --> 00:05:57,310
nothing at all. Gradient descent is very

167
00:05:57,320 --> 00:05:59,470
good at finding shortcuts like this, and

168
00:05:59,480 --> 00:06:00,990
early joint embedding methods fell

169
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:03,350
straight into it. A useful way to see

170
00:06:03,360 --> 00:06:05,470
what's missing is the energy view that

171
00:06:05,480 --> 00:06:07,710
Lacun uses. Think of the network as

172
00:06:07,720 --> 00:06:10,590
giving every pair of X and Y a number

173
00:06:10,600 --> 00:06:12,750
called its energy, which in this case is

174
00:06:12,760 --> 00:06:15,350
just the prediction error. Low when Y is

175
00:06:15,360 --> 00:06:17,710
a sensible match for X, and high when it

176
00:06:17,720 --> 00:06:20,270
isn't. Training on real pairs pushes

177
00:06:20,280 --> 00:06:22,550
their energy down. But, if nothing ever

178
00:06:22,560 --> 00:06:24,909
pushes energy up anywhere else, the

179
00:06:24,919 --> 00:06:27,230
whole surface can sink flat and low

180
00:06:27,240 --> 00:06:29,310
everywhere, and that flat surface is

181
00:06:29,320 --> 00:06:31,590
exactly the collapsed solution. There

182
00:06:31,600 --> 00:06:33,710
are two main families of fixes.

183
00:06:33,720 --> 00:06:35,830
Contrastive methods push energy up on

184
00:06:35,840 --> 00:06:37,590
purpose by showing the network

185
00:06:37,600 --> 00:06:39,830
mismatched pairs, called negatives, and

186
00:06:39,840 --> 00:06:41,550
demanding that their embeddings end up

187
00:06:41,560 --> 00:06:44,150
far apart. That works, but in a space

188
00:06:44,160 --> 00:06:46,150
with hundreds of dimensions, you need an

189
00:06:46,160 --> 00:06:48,030
enormous number of negatives to cover

190
00:06:48,040 --> 00:06:50,550
it. Regularized methods, instead, limit

191
00:06:50,560 --> 00:06:52,150
how much of the space can have low

192
00:06:52,160 --> 00:06:54,390
energy in the first place. One of them,

193
00:06:54,400 --> 00:06:56,630
called VicReg, adds penalty terms that

194
00:06:56,640 --> 00:06:58,350
keep every dimension of the embedding

195
00:06:58,360 --> 00:07:00,870
varying across a batch of examples, and

196
00:07:00,880 --> 00:07:02,630
stop different dimensions from carrying

197
00:07:02,640 --> 00:07:04,670
the same information. So, a constant

198
00:07:04,680 --> 00:07:07,270
output becomes expensive by design. The

199
00:07:07,280 --> 00:07:09,550
JEPA image and video models lean on a

200
00:07:09,560 --> 00:07:11,630
third trick, borrowed from an earlier

201
00:07:11,640 --> 00:07:15,310
method called BYOL, short for bootstrap

202
00:07:15,320 --> 00:07:17,630
your own latent. The target encoder

203
00:07:17,640 --> 00:07:19,550
isn't trained by gradient descent at

204
00:07:19,560 --> 00:07:21,909
all. Its weights are a slowly moving

205
00:07:21,919 --> 00:07:23,710
average of the context encoder's

206
00:07:23,720 --> 00:07:25,950
weights, and no gradient flows back

207
00:07:25,960 --> 00:07:27,710
through the target side, which is called

208
00:07:27,720 --> 00:07:29,950
a stop gradient. The predictor keeps

209
00:07:29,960 --> 00:07:31,750
chasing a target that shifts only

210
00:07:31,760 --> 00:07:34,110
slightly each step, and in practice, the

211
00:07:34,120 --> 00:07:36,390
system settles on rich representations

212
00:07:36,400 --> 00:07:38,790
instead of a constant. Why that's enough

213
00:07:38,800 --> 00:07:40,870
still isn't fully understood, although

214
00:07:40,880 --> 00:07:42,870
there's theory suggesting the predictor

215
00:07:42,880 --> 00:07:45,030
and the slow updates together stop the

216
00:07:45,040 --> 00:07:46,990
collapse solution from being a stable

217
00:07:47,000 --> 00:07:49,430
place to settle. It's also fragile, and

218
00:07:49,440 --> 00:07:51,070
the easiest way to see how much the

219
00:07:51,080 --> 00:07:53,230
details matter is to walk through one

220
00:07:53,240 --> 00:07:54,550
real model.

221
00:07:54,560 --> 00:07:56,470
The image JEPA model, which Meta

222
00:07:56,480 --> 00:07:59,510
published in 2023, is the image version.

223
00:07:59,520 --> 00:08:01,190
And its setup is concrete enough to

224
00:08:01,200 --> 00:08:03,710
follow step-by-step. Start with a photo

225
00:08:03,720 --> 00:08:06,910
224 pixels on a side and cut it into a

226
00:08:06,920 --> 00:08:10,030
grid of 16 by 16 pixel patches, which

227
00:08:10,040 --> 00:08:13,830
gives 14 patches across and 14 down, 196

228
00:08:13,840 --> 00:08:15,670
in all. The encoders are vision

229
00:08:15,680 --> 00:08:18,110
transformers, so each patch becomes one

230
00:08:18,120 --> 00:08:20,430
token going in, and each token comes out

231
00:08:20,440 --> 00:08:22,390
the other end as an embedding. From

232
00:08:22,400 --> 00:08:24,790
there, the model picks its targets. It

233
00:08:24,800 --> 00:08:27,030
samples four rectangular blocks, each

234
00:08:27,040 --> 00:08:29,670
covering somewhere between 15 and 20% of

235
00:08:29,680 --> 00:08:31,630
the image, with shapes ranging from a

236
00:08:31,640 --> 00:08:34,029
bit wider than tall to a bit taller than

237
00:08:34,039 --> 00:08:36,469
wide. Then, it picks a context block

238
00:08:36,479 --> 00:08:38,270
covering most of the image, anywhere

239
00:08:38,280 --> 00:08:41,270
from 85 to 100%, and cuts out every

240
00:08:41,280 --> 00:08:43,230
patch that overlaps a target, so the

241
00:08:43,240 --> 00:08:45,510
context can't just copy the answer.

242
00:08:45,520 --> 00:08:47,430
What's left is often around half the

243
00:08:47,440 --> 00:08:49,790
photo, with four holes in it. At that

244
00:08:49,800 --> 00:08:51,670
point, the three networks each do their

245
00:08:51,680 --> 00:08:54,030
job. The target encoder sees the full

246
00:08:54,040 --> 00:08:55,870
image and produces an embedding for

247
00:08:55,880 --> 00:08:57,910
every patch. And the embeddings inside

248
00:08:57,920 --> 00:08:59,510
the four target blocks become the

249
00:08:59,520 --> 00:09:02,190
answers. The context encoder sees only

250
00:09:02,200 --> 00:09:04,590
the context patches. The predictor takes

251
00:09:04,600 --> 00:09:06,470
the context embeddings plus one

252
00:09:06,480 --> 00:09:08,790
placeholder token for each hidden patch

253
00:09:08,800 --> 00:09:10,670
tagged with that patch's position, and

254
00:09:10,680 --> 00:09:12,510
outputs a predicted embedding for each

255
00:09:12,520 --> 00:09:14,870
one. The loss is the average squared

256
00:09:14,880 --> 00:09:16,790
distance between predicted and target

257
00:09:16,800 --> 00:09:19,070
embeddings across every hidden patch in

258
00:09:19,080 --> 00:09:21,270
all four blocks. Only the context

259
00:09:21,280 --> 00:09:23,430
encoder and the predictor get gradients,

260
00:09:23,440 --> 00:09:25,070
and after each step, the target

261
00:09:25,080 --> 00:09:27,230
encoder's weights move a tiny fraction

262
00:09:27,240 --> 00:09:29,270
of the way toward the context encoders,

263
00:09:29,280 --> 00:09:32,430
with a momentum that starts at 0.996

264
00:09:32,440 --> 00:09:34,870
and rises to one by the end of training.

265
00:09:34,880 --> 00:09:37,630
The The sizes turn out to matter a lot.

266
00:09:37,640 --> 00:09:39,550
Small targets can be filled in from the

267
00:09:39,560 --> 00:09:41,670
texture around them, so the network ends

268
00:09:41,680 --> 00:09:43,750
up learning low-level detail. While

269
00:09:43,760 --> 00:09:45,990
large targets can only be predicted by

270
00:09:46,000 --> 00:09:47,870
knowing something like what the object

271
00:09:47,880 --> 00:09:50,310
is and how its parts fit together. With

272
00:09:50,320 --> 00:09:52,710
large targets and a spread-out context,

273
00:09:52,720 --> 00:09:54,590
Image Jeppa learned features that did

274
00:09:54,600 --> 00:09:56,670
well on classification, on counting

275
00:09:56,680 --> 00:09:59,390
objects, and on estimating depth without

276
00:09:59,400 --> 00:10:00,870
any of the hand-designed image

277
00:10:00,880 --> 00:10:02,790
augmentations that other methods rely

278
00:10:02,800 --> 00:10:05,030
on. It also trained a huge vision

279
00:10:05,040 --> 00:10:07,590
transformer on ImageNet in under 72

280
00:10:07,600 --> 00:10:10,910
hours on 16 GPUs, several times cheaper

281
00:10:10,920 --> 00:10:13,390
than comparable methods. An image though

282
00:10:13,400 --> 00:10:15,710
is a frozen moment, and the reason LeCun

283
00:10:15,720 --> 00:10:17,910
cared about any of this was learning how

284
00:10:17,920 --> 00:10:19,750
the world moves.

285
00:10:19,760 --> 00:10:21,950
Video Jeppa brings the same recipe to

286
00:10:21,960 --> 00:10:24,710
video. A clip becomes a block of patches

287
00:10:24,720 --> 00:10:27,150
stretching across both space and time,

288
00:10:27,160 --> 00:10:29,270
and the model hides large regions,

289
00:10:29,280 --> 00:10:31,670
usually the same area in every frame, so

290
00:10:31,680 --> 00:10:33,390
it can't just copy from the frame next

291
00:10:33,400 --> 00:10:35,470
door, and then predicts the embeddings

292
00:10:35,480 --> 00:10:37,390
of what's hidden from what's left.

293
00:10:37,400 --> 00:10:39,390
Trained this way on around 2 million

294
00:10:39,400 --> 00:10:41,790
videos with no labels and no pixel

295
00:10:41,800 --> 00:10:43,750
reconstruction, the frozen encoder

296
00:10:43,760 --> 00:10:45,430
picked up motion well enough to do

297
00:10:45,440 --> 00:10:47,950
strongly on action recognition tasks

298
00:10:47,960 --> 00:10:49,510
where the only way to get the answer

299
00:10:49,520 --> 00:10:51,829
right is to understand what changed over

300
00:10:51,839 --> 00:10:54,070
time. The step that turns this into a

301
00:10:54,080 --> 00:10:56,550
world model came with Video Jeppa 2 in

302
00:10:56,560 --> 00:10:59,630
2025. Meta first pre-trained it on over

303
00:10:59,640 --> 00:11:01,790
a million hours of internet video, then

304
00:11:01,800 --> 00:11:03,750
froze the encoder and trained a new

305
00:11:03,760 --> 00:11:05,750
predictor on a small amount of robot

306
00:11:05,760 --> 00:11:08,310
data, around 62 hours of a robot arm

307
00:11:08,320 --> 00:11:10,470
moving around. This predictor takes the

308
00:11:10,480 --> 00:11:12,710
current embedding and a proposed action,

309
00:11:12,720 --> 00:11:14,030
like moving the gripper a few

310
00:11:14,040 --> 00:11:16,030
centimeters to the left and closing it,

311
00:11:16,040 --> 00:11:17,630
and predicts the embedding of what the

312
00:11:17,640 --> 00:11:19,630
camera will see afterward. That's

313
00:11:19,640 --> 00:11:22,030
exactly the Jeppa shape, with the action

314
00:11:22,040 --> 00:11:24,110
as the extra input to the predictor.

315
00:11:24,120 --> 00:11:26,030
With that in place, planning becomes a

316
00:11:26,040 --> 00:11:28,230
search. You give the robot a photo of

317
00:11:28,240 --> 00:11:30,390
the goal, say a cup sitting on a plate,

318
00:11:30,400 --> 00:11:32,670
and encode it. The robot then imagines

319
00:11:32,680 --> 00:11:34,790
many candidate sequences of actions,

320
00:11:34,800 --> 00:11:36,670
runs each one through the predictor, and

321
00:11:36,680 --> 00:11:39,030
scores it by how far its predicted final

322
00:11:39,040 --> 00:11:41,230
embedding lands from the goal embedding.

323
00:11:41,240 --> 00:11:42,910
It carries out the first action of the

324
00:11:42,920 --> 00:11:45,310
best sequence, looks again, and replans

325
00:11:45,320 --> 00:11:47,270
from there. In the energy view, it's

326
00:11:47,280 --> 00:11:48,630
picking the actions that make the

327
00:11:48,640 --> 00:11:50,430
predicted future and the goal

328
00:11:50,440 --> 00:11:52,630
compatible. Because all of this happens

329
00:11:52,640 --> 00:11:54,910
in embedding space, each imagined future

330
00:11:54,920 --> 00:11:56,870
costs a pass through a network instead

331
00:11:56,880 --> 00:11:58,910
of generating video. And Meta reported

332
00:11:58,920 --> 00:12:01,350
planning each step in seconds, where

333
00:12:01,360 --> 00:12:02,790
video generating world model took

334
00:12:02,800 --> 00:12:05,110
minutes. The robots picked up and placed

335
00:12:05,120 --> 00:12:06,670
objects in labs they'd never been

336
00:12:06,680 --> 00:12:08,750
trained in, with no rewards and no

337
00:12:08,760 --> 00:12:11,110
demonstrations for those specific tasks.

338
00:12:11,120 --> 00:12:13,230
That means a model that learned mostly

339
00:12:13,240 --> 00:12:15,550
by watching video can plan physical

340
00:12:15,560 --> 00:12:17,910
actions. It's close to the picture LaCun

341
00:12:17,920 --> 00:12:19,870
started with, but it doesn't mean the

342
00:12:19,880 --> 00:12:21,830
problem is solved, and the gaps are

343
00:12:21,840 --> 00:12:23,870
worth being precise about.

344
00:12:23,880 --> 00:12:25,910
The first gap is that you can't look at

345
00:12:25,920 --> 00:12:28,190
what a Jepa predicts. Its predictions

346
00:12:28,200 --> 00:12:30,830
are vectors, not images. So, there's no

347
00:12:30,840 --> 00:12:32,670
picture to check, and the only way to

348
00:12:32,680 --> 00:12:34,950
find out what the embedding captured is

349
00:12:34,960 --> 00:12:37,150
to train small probe networks on top of

350
00:12:37,160 --> 00:12:39,590
it and see which tasks they can solve.

351
00:12:39,600 --> 00:12:41,270
That also makes failures harder to

352
00:12:41,280 --> 00:12:43,670
diagnose, because a representation can

353
00:12:43,680 --> 00:12:45,910
quietly miss something important, like

354
00:12:45,920 --> 00:12:48,110
the exact position of a small object,

355
00:12:48,120 --> 00:12:49,430
and you'll only find out when a

356
00:12:49,440 --> 00:12:52,470
downstream task goes wrong. is that

357
00:12:52,480 --> 00:12:54,750
collapse never fully goes away. The

358
00:12:54,760 --> 00:12:56,670
moving average target, the size of the

359
00:12:56,680 --> 00:12:58,670
predictor, and the masking scheme all

360
00:12:58,680 --> 00:13:00,470
have to be balanced against each other.

361
00:13:00,480 --> 00:13:02,270
And a setup that trains well on one data

362
00:13:02,280 --> 00:13:04,750
set can degrade on another. Researchers

363
00:13:04,760 --> 00:13:06,750
are still working out the theory of why

364
00:13:06,760 --> 00:13:08,350
these systems avoid the trivial

365
00:13:08,360 --> 00:13:10,230
solution, and newer variants add

366
00:13:10,240 --> 00:13:12,390
explicit regularization rather than

367
00:13:12,400 --> 00:13:14,829
trusting the balance alone. Beyond that,

368
00:13:14,839 --> 00:13:17,070
the choice of what to hide is itself a

369
00:13:17,080 --> 00:13:19,430
choice about what counts as important.

370
00:13:19,440 --> 00:13:21,630
Large image blocks push the model toward

371
00:13:21,640 --> 00:13:23,710
objects, and hiding the same region

372
00:13:23,720 --> 00:13:26,190
across frames pushes it toward motion.

373
00:13:26,200 --> 00:13:28,230
But a person picked those rules. The

374
00:13:28,240 --> 00:13:30,270
robot results are real, but they're for

375
00:13:30,280 --> 00:13:32,910
short tasks with a goal photo. LeCun's

376
00:13:32,920 --> 00:13:35,030
full proposal stacks Jeppas into a

377
00:13:35,040 --> 00:13:37,190
hierarchy where the lower levels predict

378
00:13:37,200 --> 00:13:39,070
the next fraction of a second in fine

379
00:13:39,080 --> 00:13:41,190
detail, and the higher levels predict

380
00:13:41,200 --> 00:13:43,390
minutes ahead in abstract terms. So,

381
00:13:43,400 --> 00:13:45,190
that a system could plan something like

382
00:13:45,200 --> 00:13:46,790
a trip to the airport. And that

383
00:13:46,800 --> 00:13:48,990
hierarchy is still mostly a research

384
00:13:49,000 --> 00:13:51,550
plan. Jeppa also isn't a replacement for

385
00:13:51,560 --> 00:13:53,110
language models since it doesn't

386
00:13:53,120 --> 00:13:55,150
generate text or images at all. And

387
00:13:55,160 --> 00:13:56,870
whether it beats generative training at

388
00:13:56,880 --> 00:13:59,030
very large scale is still being argued

389
00:13:59,040 --> 00:13:59,950
out.

390
00:13:59,960 --> 00:14:01,870
What Jeppa does settle is the question

391
00:14:01,880 --> 00:14:03,670
from the start about what a machine

392
00:14:03,680 --> 00:14:05,190
should predict when it learns by

393
00:14:05,200 --> 00:14:07,950
watching. Predicting every pixel forces

394
00:14:07,960 --> 00:14:09,830
a model to care about everything,

395
00:14:09,840 --> 00:14:11,990
including what's random. Predicting in

396
00:14:12,000 --> 00:14:14,230
an embedding space lets the model decide

397
00:14:14,240 --> 00:14:15,990
what's worth representing, and then

398
00:14:16,000 --> 00:14:18,350
holds it to predicting that part well.

399
00:14:18,360 --> 00:14:19,910
That's what the one-year-old is doing

400
00:14:19,920 --> 00:14:21,350
when they look to the far end of the

401
00:14:21,360 --> 00:14:23,150
couch because they aren't predicting the

402
00:14:23,160 --> 00:14:24,630
pattern on the carpet, they're

403
00:14:24,640 --> 00:14:27,510
predicting where the car will come out.

404
